cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "FusionFlux: extrapolation, long-range saturation and physics constraints in confinement scaling"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Liam
    family-names: Salcedo
    alias: lsalcedo100
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-5039-8147"
repository-code: "https://github.com/lsalcedo100/FusionFlux"
abstract: >-
  Confinement scaling laws are fitted to existing tokamaks and used to size
  ones not yet built, so what matters is accuracy on an unseen device. On
  the ITPA Global H-mode Confinement Database (6228 time slices, 16
  devices), cross-validation that holds out discharges ranks a random forest
  29% more accurate than a refitted power law. Holding out whole devices
  reverses the ranking: the forest is worse than the power law on all 11
  devices with enough data to score. The reversal survives, though not on
  every device, when stored energy replaces confinement time as the target
  and when the features are replaced by dimensionless groups that carry less
  device identity. It reproduces on all 7 eligible devices of the
  stellarator-heliotron confinement database, in an analysis fixed before
  those data were downloaded. At a size cut matching ITER's 1.82-fold step
  in major radius, 90% split-conformal intervals cover 3% and 0% of held-out
  rows for the two tree ensembles, with widths that change by under 1.5%, so
  they give no sign of failing. Connor-Taylor constraints on the exponents
  and a Gaussian process with a linear kernel term both improve
  extrapolation. An ablation that varies only the long-range mean points to
  saturation, rather than flexibility alone, as the cause in the models
  tested. Four reporting checks follow, three of which need no extra
  fitting.
  Includes a rank-deficiency audit of the design matrix, a from-scratch
  least-squares implementation (Cholesky, QR, SVD), a refit of the
  IPB98(y,2) exponents decomposed along the singular directions of the data,
  and a domain-agnostic module packaging the audit for other scaling-law
  datasets.
keywords:
  - fusion
  - tokamak
  - plasma physics
  - energy confinement time
  - scaling law
  - machine learning
  - extrapolation
  - out-of-distribution generalization
  - Gaussian processes
  - allometry
  - model selection
